# A task tracker's password reset spends a token out of a token store that
# keys every row by a kind: `getToken(s, reset.Token, TokenPasswordReset)`
# reads the row the mailed token names (pkg/user/user_password_reset.go:48 at
# the parent of the change), `HashPassword` writes the new credential, and
# `removeTokens(s, user, TokenEmailConfirm)` then deletes the user's rows of a
# *different* kind (user_password_reset.go:68). The reset token the request
# authenticated with is never the one removed, so it stays in the store after
# use and keeps authorising resets -- including after the password it
# issued for has been changed (CWE-613, insufficient expiration of a
# single-use token). The fixed revision changes one constant: the same call with
# `TokenPasswordReset`.
#
# The two sibling flows in the same package show what the shape shares:
# `ConfirmEmailAddress` (pkg/user/user_email_confirm.go:37/:51) and the
# account-deletion confirm (pkg/user/delete.go:115/:133) each call getToken
# and removeTokens with the *same* kind, and are correct. Every function in
# the family carries the identical call pair, so the call pair alone carries
# no information about the defect.
#
# Measured on both revisions of the tree, three `--cache off` runs each:
# 95 findings at the parent, 96 at the fixed revision; the +1 is the fixed revision's own added
# regression-test block shifting lines in user_test.go. Nothing lands on
# pkg/user/user_password_reset.go in either revision, and `vyql match` on the
# file labels nothing at all -- no source, no sink, no control. The request
# token flows into a parameterized query in both revisions, so there is no
# taint route: the defect is which enum constant names the removal's kind.
#
# Two probe bindings measured the expression ceiling:
#
# (a) The generic pair -- a Go function whose context carries both `getToken`
#     and `removeTokens` -- fires on all three functions of the family:
#     ResetPassword, ConfirmEmailAddress, and the deletion confirm. Three
#     findings on the parent, the same three on the fixed revision, two of the three on
#     correctly-matched code. No discrimination, wrong in both directions.
#
# (b) Naming the constant pair -- the function's identifiers contain both
#     `TokenPasswordReset` and `TokenEmailConfirm` -- discriminates 1 -> 0,
#     and the rename test kills it: renaming the constant `TokenEmailConfirm`
#     to `TokenMailConfirm` in the vulnerable source (a change with no
#     semantic effect) takes the finding to 0. It keys on the fixing commit's
#     own vocabulary, and the vocabulary is the application's private enum
#     (pkg/user/token.go:33-37), not a documented library API -- no other
#     tree among the corpus clones carries any of it.
#
# What would actually discriminate is a relation the binding language does
# not have: *the kind argument of the removal equals the kind argument of the
# lookup*. Predicates match a node's tokens against literal values; no
# predicate unifies two token atoms, within one node or across two call
# nodes. So every absence-shaped binding of the
# code.PasswordResetTokenReplay family points the wrong way here as well:
# its negations treat recovery-store vocabulary (`GetByToken`, `DeleteToken`,
# ...) as the revocable, safe form, and this function *is* the revocable form
# -- the lookup, the removal call and the store are all present; only the
# constant at the removal is wrong.
#
# Cases: 1 is the control -- the salt-recomputation shape the shipped
# goPasswordResetTokenReplay binding covers still fires in Go, so the miss is
# this shape, not a dead rule. 2 pins the miss on the real vulnerable shape
# and flips only if a cross-call kind-equality relation ever becomes
# expressible. 3 is the patched form and 4 the correctly-matched sibling;
# both must stay reject under any future capability, because the call pair
# and the store vocabulary are identical in the correct code.

test "rank1918: password reset recomputing the token from a configured salt still reports"
  lang go
  file reset.go
  expect VYQL-AUTH-117
  expect_review code.PasswordResetTokenReplay
  code
  ```
  package user

  func ResetPasswordFromSalt(userID string, newPassword string, data string, hash string) error {
  	if !ComparePassword(hash, data+PasswordResetSalt) {
  		return ErrInvalidLink
  	}
  	return UpdatePassword(userID, HashPassword(newPassword))
  }
  ```

test "rank1918: password reset removes a token of a different kind than the one it spent"
  lang go
  file reset.go
  reject VYQL-AUTH-117
  reject_review code.PasswordResetTokenReplay
  code
  ```
  package user

  import "xorm.io/xorm"

  type PasswordReset struct {
  	// The previously issued reset token.
  	Token string `json:"token"`
  	// The new password for this user.
  	NewPassword string `json:"new_password"`
  }

  func ResetPassword(s *xorm.Session, reset *PasswordReset) (userID int64, err error) {
  	if reset.NewPassword == "" {
  		return 0, ErrNoUsernamePassword{}
  	}

  	if reset.Token == "" {
  		return 0, ErrNoPasswordResetToken{}
  	}

  	// Check if we have a token
  	token, err := getToken(s, reset.Token, TokenPasswordReset)
  	if err != nil {
  		return 0, err
  	}
  	if token == nil {
  		return 0, ErrInvalidPasswordResetToken{Token: reset.Token}
  	}

  	user, err := GetUserByID(s, token.UserID)
  	if err != nil {
  		return
  	}
  	userID = user.ID

  	// Hash the password
  	user.Password, err = HashPassword(reset.NewPassword)
  	if err != nil {
  		return
  	}

  	err = removeTokens(s, user, TokenEmailConfirm)
  	if err != nil {
  		return
  	}

  	_, err = s.
  		Cols("password", "status").
  		Where("id = ?", user.ID).
  		Update(user)
  	return
  }
  ```

